anna (devex): quick question — hitting a couple related problems while integrating func/tool calls in the TS SDK. TL;DR: streaming chunks + schema typing + auth retries are interacting badly. Anyone else seen this?

mike (eng-sdk): yeah seen variations. can you paste the minimal failure pattern?

anna (devex): repro steps: call tool that streams partial 'result' chunks and then finalizes with a JSON payload. sometimes the final payload omits optional keys entirely, and our strict TS interface expects them (we use non-null assertions in a few places). also, if token refresh happens mid-stream we get a 401 and the client retries the request — which sometimes causes duplicate chunk handling in the app layer.

anna (devex): example final payloads (both valid by runtime):
```json
{ "tool": "db_query", "rows": null }
{ "tool": "db_query" } // rows missing entirely
```

mike (eng-sdk): ugh. missing vs null is always messy across languages. short answers: 1) we should not assume non-null for optional fields in SDK runtime types, 2) introduce a small normalization helper that canonicalizes missing->undefined and null->undefined (or vice versa consistently per SDK), 3) for streaming+retry, make retries idempotent at transport level (replay suppression) and have the SDK expose a per-stream request id so apps can dedupe.

mike (eng-sdk): quick TS helper sketch:
```ts
export function normalizeToolOutput<T>(raw: any): Partial<T> {
  // coerce explicit nulls to undefined, drop __internal keys, etc.
  const out: any = {};
  for (const k of Object.keys(raw || {})) {
    const v = raw[k];
    out[k] = v === null ? undefined : v;
  }
  return out as Partial<T>
}
```

charles (eng-runtime): for the retry duplicate-chunk problem, runtime can add a small per-request monotonic stream-id and sequence numbers on chunks. transport retries then include the original stream-id and start-seq so receiver can ignore already-seen seqs. We already have request-id metadata — might be enough if SDK surfaces it.

ravi (eng-ml): for Go and strongly-typed clients it's trickier. pattern we've used: generate pointer fields for optionals and implement custom Unmarshal that treats missing/null the same. also provide a small `NormalizeToolOutput` helper for consumers that unwraps pointers into nil/zero values or keeps pointers when you want to see exactly what was present.

ravi (eng-ml): example Go idea:
```go
type ToolResult struct {
  Tool string `json:\"tool\"`
  Rows *[]Row `json:\"rows,omitempty\"` // nil if missing or null
}
func Normalize(r ToolResult) {
  if r.Rows == nil {
    // treat missing/null same way or set to empty slice depending on API promise
  }
}
```

liz (qa): we should have tests for: missing vs null, multiple retries during streaming, and end-to-end app-level dedupe behavior. i'll add a checklist to the PR. also recommend adding a small fuzz test for schema permutations.

anna (devex): sounds good. I'm going to open a PR with: 1) TS normalize helper + docs, 2) SDK-level stream-id exposure + doc on how to dedupe, 3) e2e tests in qa matrix. pinging for reviewers: @mike, @charles, @ravi, @liz.

mike (eng-sdk): happy to review. one note: let's make helper name `normalizeToolOutputForSdk` to be explicit. also add a runtime-backcompat note that older clients may expect nulls.

charles (eng-runtime): will review the stream-id bits; if you want I can add server-side seq metadata so SDK changes are minimal.

anna (devex): awesome — I'll split the PR into two: SDK changes + tests, and runtime seq-id followup. keeping them separate to make rollout easier.

liz (qa): ping me on the PR when ready. I'll wire the e2e.

